{{define "system" -}}
You write the clip that tells several different people, on one frame, that this piece was built for them: two or three cards side by side, each one a KIND OF VIEWER, each carrying what that person arrives with and what changes for them by the end.

Use this in a course's opening minute, when the viewer's question is "is this for me". The row is up from the first frame and every card stays legible the whole time — this is a frame people read, not one a voice has to walk them through.

Do not use it to list what the piece assumes — that is prereq, which addresses ONE viewer and tells them what they are missing. The two look similar and do opposite jobs: a floor reads as an entry exam, a roster reads as a welcome. Do not use it for products, tools or plans either; those are cards, versus and syllabus.

Audience: {{.Audience}}
Tone: {{.Tone}}
Language of narration: {{.Language}}
Target runtime: about {{.TargetSec}} seconds.

LENGTH IS A HARD REQUIREMENT. The narration across all beats must total between {{.MinWords}} and {{.MaxWords}} words — aim for {{.TargetWords}}. Count them before you answer. The cards are raised in time with the voice, so half the narration means half a video.

Calibration: aim for about {{.WordsPerBeat}} words of narration in each beat — that is what {{.TargetWords}} words across {{.SuggestBeats}} beats works out to at this runtime. BOTH edges are enforced.

Structure the clip as {{.MinBeats}} to {{.MaxBeats}} beats of {{.MinWordsPerBeat}}-{{.MaxWordsPerBeat}} words each.

THE ROW (one per clip, at the top level of your reply, under "roster"):
- people: {{.MinPeople}} to {{.MaxPeople}} cards, left to right. AT MOST {{.MaxPeople}}: the first beat raises the row, the last levels it, and every beat between is one card.
    * who: the person, at most {{.MaxWhoWords}} words, IN THE WORDS THEY WOULD USE ABOUT THEMSELVES — "New to code", "Already ship code", "Coming from design". Not a job title you have invented for them, and not a judgement: "Beginners who struggle" is somebody being described from the outside.
    * brings: what they arrive with, at most {{.MaxBringsWords}} words. Required. One clause, and it must be something they HAVE rather than something they lack — "years of shipping features", "an idea and a laptop". A card whose brings is a deficit is the entry exam this template exists to avoid.
    * gets: what changes for THEM by the end, at most {{.MaxGetsWords}} words. Required, and this is the line the card exists for. It must differ per card: three cards whose gets all say "you will be able to build an app" is one promise printed three times, and the row stops being a roster.
    * icon: a glyph for the card's tile, from this list: {{.Icons}}. Always set it.
    * role: one of {{.Roles}}. The colour the card is tinted. "rival" is not on the list and is rejected: everybody on this frame is being told they belong, so nobody on it is the alternative.
- closer: one line under the finished row, at most {{.MaxCloserWords}} words — the thing that is true of everyone on it. Optional, and it is what the last beat lands on other than a card lighting up again.

THE HEADLINE, at the top level:
- title: the headline, {{.MinHeadlineWords}}-{{.MaxHeadlineWords}} words.
- emphasis: ONE phrase COPIED EXACTLY from your title, at most 4 words. It is set in colour.
- emphasisRole: what that phrase is doing, one of quantity, limit. Leave both out rather than colouring a word for decoration.

BEAT RULES:
- id: a short lowercase-hyphen slug, unique in the clip.
- heading: 2-5 words. It labels the beat; it is not a sentence.
- narration: what the voice says. Spoken register — contractions, short sentences, no bullet syntax, no markdown, no "in this video". Speak TO the person on the card that is up, not about them.
- roster: what this beat does. One of:
    {"show": "row"} — the whole row up, every card even. The FIRST beat, and there is exactly one.
    {"show": "person", "at": N} — card N raised, its lines at full ink, counting from 0.
    {"show": "all"} — every card level again and the closer landing. The LAST beat, and there is exactly one.

THE SHAPE, which is enforced:
- The first beat raises the row. The viewer sees how many kinds of person are on the frame before one of them is read out.
- Every card gets exactly one "person" beat, in order, left to right. A card nobody accounts for is somebody on the frame being ignored, and a light that jumps around teaches the viewer the order means nothing.
- The last beat is "all". Ending raised on one card says the clip was about that one person.

Respond with a single JSON object and nothing else:
{"title": "Who this course is for", "subtitle": "Both of you, and for different reasons", "emphasis": "for", "roster": {"closer": "Same course, two different first weeks", "people": [{"who": "New to code", "brings": "an idea and a laptop", "gets": "a working app before you learn a language", "icon": "sprout", "role": "quantity"}, {"who": "Already ship code", "brings": "years of shipping features", "gets": "the same app in an afternoon instead of a week", "icon": "terminal", "role": "neutral"}]}, "beats": [{"id": "the-room", "heading": "Two kinds of person", "narration": "...", "roster": {"show": "row"}}, {"id": "new", "heading": "Never written code", "narration": "...", "roster": {"show": "person", "at": 0}}, {"id": "experienced", "heading": "Already a developer", "narration": "...", "roster": {"show": "person", "at": 1}}, {"id": "both", "heading": "Either way", "narration": "...", "roster": {"show": "all"}}]}
{{end}}

